   source:'202 F26 Memory II.pptx · slides 3–9, 12 + '+background,
   body:[
    'Loftus’s misinformation research shows the result the slides set up. In the stop/yield study everyone saw a stop sign. Participants who later read a misleading account mentioning a yield sign were more likely to pick the yield sign on the recognition test than those given consistent information. Information encountered after the event became part of the memory.',
    'In Loftus and Palmer (1974), people watched a car crash and estimated speed. Asking how fast the cars were going when they “smashed” produced higher estimates than “hit”. A week later, the “smashed” group was more likely to report broken glass that was never in the film. Question wording alone altered the memory.',
    'Steven Avery was convicted in 1985 of the assault on Penny Beerntsen, largely on her sincere, confident identification. The real attacker, Gregory Allen, was never in the lineup. Avery served 18 years before DNA evidence exonerated him in 2003 and identified Allen. Mistaken eyewitness identification contributes to most DNA exonerations in the United States.',
    'Weapon focus shows emotion shaping memory. When a weapon is visible, witnesses look at it more and describe it in more detail, but they are worse at describing or identifying the person holding it. Attention narrows onto the threat.',
    'Overall answer to “how accurate is retrieval?”: memory is reconstructive. It can be mostly right about gist yet wrong on details, and confidence or vividness does not guarantee accuracy, especially after suggestive questions or repeated exposure to a suspect.'
   ],
   terms:[
    {term:'Misinformation effect',definition:'Misleading information after an event alters memory for it, as in the stop/yield and smashed/hit studies.'},
    {term:'Avery exoneration',definition:'A confident eyewitness identified the wrong man; DNA cleared Avery after 18 years and identified Gregory Allen.'},
    {term:'Weapon focus',definition:'Attention to a visible weapon improves memory for the weapon but impairs memory for the perpetrator’s face.'}
   ],
